Here's a **MinMaxStack** that tracks both minimum and maximum in O(1):

```java
public class MinMaxStack<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    private static class Node<T> {
        T value;
        T minAtLevel;
        T maxAtLevel;

        Node(T value, T minAtLevel, T maxAtLevel) {
            this.value = value;
            this.minAtLevel = minAtLevel;
            this.maxAtLevel = maxAtLevel;
        }
    }

    private List<Node<T>> stack;

    public MinMaxStack() {
        this.stack = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void push(T value) {
        T newMin = value;
        T newMax = value;

        if (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            Node<T> top = stack.get(stack.size() - 1);
            if (value.compareTo(top.minAtLevel) > 0) {
                newMin = top.minAtLevel;
            }
            if (value.compareTo(top.maxAtLevel) < 0) {
                newMax = top.maxAtLevel;
            }
        }

        stack.add(new Node<>(value, newMin, newMax));
    }

    public T pop() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.remove(stack.size() - 1).value;
    }

    public MinMax<T> getMinMax() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        Node<T> top = stack.get(stack.size() - 1);
        return new MinMax<>(top.minAtLevel, top.maxAtLevel);
    }

    public static class MinMax<T> {
        public final T min;
        public final T max;

        MinMax(T min, T max) {
            this.min = min;
            this.max = max;
        }
    }
}
```

**Why min/max?** It's the most universally useful constant-time enrichment — useful for range queries, outlier detection, and bounds checking. Each node stores the min and max of all elements from the bottom of the stack through that node, so querying the top gives you the answer in O(1) with O(n) space.

