Here's a stack implementation with O(1) **running product** tracking:

```java
public class ProductStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;
        long product;  // product of all elements from bottom to this node

        Node(int value, long product) {
            this.value = value;
            this.product = product;
        }
    }

    private Node top;
    private static final long PRODUCT_OVERFLOW = Long.MAX_VALUE;

    public void push(int value) {
        long newProduct;
        if (top == null) {
            newProduct = value;
        } else {
            // Guard against overflow by capping at sentinel
            if (Math.abs(top.product) > PRODUCT_OVERFLOW / Math.max(1, Math.abs(value))) {
                newProduct = PRODUCT_OVERFLOW;
            } else {
                newProduct = top.product * value;
            }
        }

        Node node = new Node(value, newProduct);
        node.next = top;
        top = node;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        int value = top.value;
        top = top.next;
        return value;
    }

    public long getProduct() {
        if (top == null) {
            return 1;  // empty stack product is identity
        }
        return top.product;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return top == null;
    }
}
```

**Why this feature?**

Running product is more interesting than min/max (which are common) or sum (trivial in this context). It's genuinely useful for problems involving geometric growth rates or combinatorial counting, and it creates a nice tension: you must track the product state through the stack structure itself rather than maintaining a simple scalar.

**Complexity guarantee:**
- **push(value)**: O(1) — single node allocation, one multiplication
- **pop()**: O(1) — pointer reassignment
- **getProduct()**: O(1) — read cached product from top node

Each node carries its cumulative product, so querying is instant without traversal.

