Here's an implementation with a **product-tracking feature** (most interesting to combine with stack operations):

```java
public class ProductStack {
    private static final int INITIAL_CAPACITY = 16;
    private int[] values;
    private int size;
    private long product;  // tracks product of all elements
    private int zeroCount; // tracks zeros to handle product = 0 case

    public ProductStack() {
        values = new int[INITIAL_CAPACITY];
        size = 0;
        product = 1;
        zeroCount = 0;
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        if (size == values.length) {
            resize();
        }

        values[size++] = value;

        if (value == 0) {
            zeroCount++;
        } else {
            product *= value;
        }
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }

        int value = values[--size];

        if (value == 0) {
            zeroCount--;
        } else {
            product /= value;
        }

        return value;
    }

    public long getProduct() {
        if (zeroCount > 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        return product;
    }

    private void resize() {
        int[] newValues = new int[values.length * 2];
        System.arraycopy(values, 0, newValues, 0, values.length);
        values = newValues;
    }
}
```

**Why product tracking?** It's non-obvious but practical (useful for probability chains, geometric means), and the zero-handling trick keeps it genuinely O(1) — no rebuilds needed on pop, just division. All three operations (push, pop, getProduct) are constant-time.

