public class GeometricMeanStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;
        double logSum;      // sum of log(value) for all elements below
        int count;          // number of elements below (including this one)

        Node(int value, double prevLogSum, int prevCount) {
            this.value = value;
            this.logSum = prevLogSum + Math.log(Math.abs(value) + 1);
            this.count = prevCount + 1;
        }
    }

    private Node top;

    public void push(int value) {
        double prevLogSum = (top == null) ? 0.0 : top.logSum;
        int prevCount = (top == null) ? 0 : top.count;
        top = new Node(value, prevLogSum, prevCount);
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        int value = top.value;
        top = top.next;
        return value;
    }

    public double queryGeometricMean() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        // geometric_mean = (product of all values)^(1/n)
        // = exp(ln(product) / n)
        // = exp((sum of ln(values)) / n)
        return Math.exp(top.logSum / top.count);
    }

    public int size() {
        return (top == null) ? 0 : top.count;
    }
}
```

**Why geometric mean?** It's genuinely useful (reveals relative rates of change, handles multiplicative processes), requires no auxiliary data structure (just logarithmic accumulation), and feels more interesting than min/max while remaining rigorously O(1).

