**Implementation: MinMaxStack**

I'll implement a stack with O(1) **min/max pairs query** — returns both minimum and maximum in the current stack simultaneously, which is more interesting than either alone.

```java
public class MinMaxStack<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    private static class Entry<T> {
        T value;
        T minVal;
        T maxVal;

        Entry(T value, T minVal, T maxVal) {
            this.value = value;
            this.minVal = minVal;
            this.maxVal = maxVal;
        }
    }

    private final java.util.ArrayDeque<Entry<T>> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();

    /**
     * Push element onto stack. O(1) time, O(1) space.
     */
    public void push(T value) {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            stack.push(new Entry<>(value, value, value));
        } else {
            Entry<T> top = stack.peek();
            T newMin = value.compareTo(top.minVal) < 0 ? value : top.minVal;
            T newMax = value.compareTo(top.maxVal) > 0 ? value : top.maxVal;
            stack.push(new Entry<>(value, newMin, newMax));
        }
    }

    /**
     * Pop and return top element. O(1) time.
     */
    public T pop() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new java.util.EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.pop().value;
    }

    /**
     * Query current min and max in O(1). Returns array [min, max].
     */
    public T[] getMinMax() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new java.util.EmptyStackException();
        }
        Entry<T> top = stack.peek();
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        T[] result = (T[]) new Comparable[]{top.minVal, top.maxVal};
        return result;
    }
}
```

**Design insight:** Each stack entry caches the min and max of all elements below it (inclusive). When pushing, we compute new min/max by comparing the incoming value against the previous top's cached bounds — no traversal needed. When popping, the bounds vanish with that entry, so the next entry's cached bounds are immediately valid.
